-- title:
--   Descent of invertibility of ideal sheaves along finite flat surjections
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes and let $\pi \colon Y \to X$ be a morphism which is finite, flat and surjective, and let $I$ be quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data on $X$ (an `IdealSheafData`, i.e. a compatible family of ideals $I.\mathrm{ideal}(U) \subseteq \Gamma(X, U)$ on affine opens). Suppose that the inverse-image ideal sheaf data $I.\mathrm{comap}\ \pi$ on $Y$ is invertible in the sense of the predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $y \in Y$ there are an affine open $V$ of $Y$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(Y, V)$ with $y \in Y.\mathrm{basicOpen}\ f$, together with an element $g$ of $\Gamma(Y, Y.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\ f)$ which is a nonzerodivisor in that ring, such that the ideal attached to $I.\mathrm{comap}\ \pi$ on the affine open $Y.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\ f$ is the principal ideal generated by $g$. The conclusion is that $I$ itself is invertible in the same sense: each $x \in X$ admits an affine open $U$ of $X$ and $f \in \Gamma(X, U)$ with $x \in X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\ f$, and a nonzerodivisor $g \in \Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\ f)$ with $I.\mathrm{ideal}(X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\ f) = (g)$.
--
--   This is faithfully flat descent for the property of being an invertible ideal sheaf, i.e. of cutting out an effective Cartier divisor: affine-locally $\pi$ is given by a finite faithfully flat algebra $A \to B$, the inverse image of $I$ is $IB$, and both finite generation and local principality with nonzerodivisor generator descend from $B$ to $A$. It is used in the construction of relative effective Cartier divisors on curves, via [`AlgebraicGeometry.RelEffCartierDiv.isInvertible_I`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.RelEffCartierDiv.isInvertible_I).
